A||N||7||PatrickB||The Fiero Blues||||9||
Z||000000||PatrickB||10-19-2000||08:26 PM||||My Fiero has just not been cooperating with me these last couple of weeks.  Tonight, I get stuck on the side of the highway with about a 5 mile walk to get some help.  Luckily that 5 miles was to my friends house and we were able to get the car back home.  <P>Heres the deal.  I was driving on the highway and everything was perfect when bang.  I hear what sounded like a backfire and I saw black smoke come from the back of my car for a second and the car dies.  I get it pulled over to check it out.  Dont see anything standing out or noticeable.  I try to start it again and it just turns over.  Now I can smell gas inside the compartment and a little underneath the car but none near the engine. Thats about it.<P>I didnt have time to check it out because it got dark out so I will look first thing tomorrow morning.   I am thinking either a fuel leak somewhere or something with the spark.  Maybe a bad coil, rotor, etc...Anyone have any ideas?<BR>||||reg||9
Z||000001||Old Lar||10-19-2000||08:52 PM||||Ignition module?||||reg||1
Z||000002||Raydar||10-19-2000||10:19 PM||||If it's not the ignition module, it could be the coil. Also check the two connectors on top of the coil. Make sure they're plugged on, tight. One goes to the distributor, the other goes into the engine harness, between the valve cover and the intake runner. Also could be a shorted tach filter. Before it died, had the tach started to jump around? If so, you can just unplug the filter (looks like an old ignition condensor). Should be a white plastic connector. Engine will run without it, but the tach won't read anything.||||reg||1
Z||000003||PatrickB||10-20-2000||10:41 AM||||The tach wasnt jumping around.  At least not that I know of.  Im going out to take a look now so hopefully I can find whats wrong.  The ignition module and the coil were the two things that I was thinking of too last night.  Didnt think of the tach filter, ill try that too.||||reg||1
Z||000004||PatrickB||10-20-2000||07:18 PM||||Woohoo got it fixed.  It turned out to be a combo of both the coil and module.  I dont know if they can knock each other out but something happened.  I tested my coil first and that didnt look so great so I got a new one.  I got one the NAPA ones so we will see how it holds up.  The module was a Borg Warner from Pep Boys.  I also changed the cap and rotor while at was at it.  I got one heck of a spark now.||||reg||6
Z||000005||theogre||10-20-2000||07:21 PM||||A failed coil can definatly fry a module.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Old Lar||10-20-2000||07:39 PM||||Glad you got it fixed.  The education we get when troubleshooting the problems are resolved.<P>I know that when my car acts that way just what to look for as a first step.||||reg||10
Z||000007||PatrickB||10-20-2000||09:19 PM||||Just something I noticed with NAPA. I got my ignition coil part #IC29SB from the local store for $49.90.  Now I just looked it up on their website and the price for the same item  is $24.99.  Half the price? I know some businesses are a little cheaper on their website than on their store to attract customers but half the price?<BR>Hmm...||||reg||1
